 "mundo": "campo",
 "autor": "claude",
 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "How does a farm windmill pull water from deep underground, with no motor at all?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Most people think it makes electricity. It doesn't.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In the Argentine pampas, these windmills are called molinos, and they're everywhere.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The wheel has up to eighteen steel blades, angled like a fan.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Even a light breeze is enough to turn it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Behind the wheel, a crank turns the spin into an up-and-down motion.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That motion moves a long steel rod, straight down into the well.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "At the bottom, below the water table, the rod pulls a piston inside a cylinder.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "On the way up, the piston lifts the water above it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A one-way valve stops it from falling back down.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Stroke after stroke, the water climbs the pipe to the surface.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It flows into a storage tank, so the cows can drink on days with no wind.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The tail keeps the wheel facing the wind, like a weather vane.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And in a storm, a spring folds the wheel sideways, so it doesn't tear itself apart.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Between eighteen fifty and nineteen thirty, America installed millions of them.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now you know.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
